WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:11.965
Hey everyone. I'm Mihaela.

00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:13.800
I'm a research engineer at DeepMind.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.359
Hi my name is Feryal. I'm a
research scientist at DeepMind.

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:19.693
I'm also on the board of Women in
Machine Learning

00:00:19.693 --> 00:00:20.720
my name is Kate Parkyn

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.080
Ii'm a recruitment lead
and I look after all engineering and

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.119
research recruitment

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:30.800
I'm generally a very curious person and
I like to

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.399
tinker and think about things that might
come up next

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:38.239
and the new possibilities for the
machine learning fields are

00:00:38.239 --> 00:00:40.780
very very exciting at the moment.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.000
I feel like my research is something that I

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.820
would do as a hobby if I had another job.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.879
It played to my geeky side, It's pretty

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.300
phenomenal what we're trying...

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.920
what we can do and what we

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.320
can leverage through artificial
intelligence and machine learning.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.060
I had a lot of doubts whether I, sort
of, am suited to join DeepMind.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.280
I guess many women and minorities 
feel the same

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.479
because we don't see a lot of us around.

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:08.960
I was indeed often questioning myself 
as in terms of

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.060
oh, should you apply

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.800
do you think you will be good enough?

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:15.600
While internally you have these

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.600
emotional reactions,  
the objective reality is that

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.540
there's an interview process
there to decide

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.740
whether someone is good enough.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.920
Optimise your CV to the best that you

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.799
can ensuring that you kind of really
pull out those

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:35.119
technical skills at the top of your CV, 
you're really getting across

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:39.119
your background, your skills, your key
achievements. I think networking does

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:41.840
have its advantages and I definitely
encourage

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.380
women to network as much as possible.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.399
I would highly recommend

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:50.240
refreshing your basics understandings of
machine learning, computer science.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.240
Focus on breadth rather than depth. If
you're researching just make sure that

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.360
you know your basics for
linear algebra, calculus, probability

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.040
you can choose which language 
you want to code in

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:04.719
I chose Python, some people choose C++

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:05.940
it's quite flexible

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.399
If there's anything
in addition that you want to tell us or

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:11.039
really highlight, 
you know, cover letters are lovely to

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:16.080
read because ultimately they are
getting across someone's real passion

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.240
and desire to want to work for DeepMind.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.520
You know we're not putting people in an

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.440
interview setting
to find out reasons not to recruit them.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.800
We want to put them in a comfortable
setting to find out the best reasons to

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.360
recruit them

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.480
I think during the interview process I
was very conscious of,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.540
I guess, being a woman,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.360
not having done an internship 
at DeepMind before.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.160
I felt like I maybe don't belong.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.560
But throughout the 
interview process I think

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.160
after like 
talking to researchers,  and

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.040
seeing that I can hold the conversation

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.440
and I can sort of answer

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.120
questions i felt more confident.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.800
I actually went in with my

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:57.920
three-month-old son.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.200
They just understood 
instantly, you know

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.280
where I was at in terms of 
being a first-time mum,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.460
on maternity, very sleep deprived.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.480
So that really helped, kind of, 
eliminate some of

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.879
the self-doubt that I was having around
my own

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:13.100
ability and confidence

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:18.640
was actually the
supportive nature of the team

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.080
In the machine learning team, this is
of one of the most

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.240
inclusive teams that 
I've ever worked in.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.480
It's just a very great 
atmosphere of

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.520
overall growth.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.879
And actually what's quite
organic about DeepMind is actually this

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:34.560
this sort of natural mentoring that
takes place

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.200
and that's amongst everybody.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.480
I certainly think that certain
 organisations such as

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.720
WiML, that allow women
 to get together and discuss both

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.519
technical challenges that they face in
terms of research but also

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:51.280
personal challenges in terms of
representation and and so on are very

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.700
very important

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.200
You do not need a PhD to work at DeepMind.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.060
I did not have a PhD 
when I joined DeepMind,

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:01.640
I do not have a PhD now.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.040
You really don't need a 
PhD necessarily

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.340
Of course that depends on the

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.480
role that
you're applying for

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.060
I think only for research scientists
role,

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:12.980
you definitely need to have a PhD

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.080
You need to apply.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.760
Just apply.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.960
Don't wait for
you to feel like something

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.540
needs to click.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.880
If you are good enough, 
if you've done

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.120
the work, 
if you are confident in your skills

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:31.900
just apply.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.680
If you get that tingly moment
when you're reading up about us

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.120
or we become that kind of niggle in

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.479
the back of your head,
 if you're well,

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:42.240
if you know if you know our research,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.080
you should definitely,

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:55.680
you're definitely ready.

